{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"59ed2da2b5411acd","name":"card_event_the_big_three","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/USA/Homefront/events/card_event_the_big_three","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"THE BIG THREE","text":"Each friendly unit gets +1+1 for each friendly unit not of the same nation.","flavorText":"The Big Three were the principal Allied leaders of WWII: Franklin D. Roosevelt, President of the USA, Winston Churchill, Prime Minister of the United Kingdom, and Joseph Stalin,General Secretary of the Soviet Union. From 1941, when the US entered the war, Roosevelt pushed for unity among the Allies despite differing political systems and postwar goals. His leadership helped shape the strategic alliance, particularly during the Tehran Conference in 1943 and the Yalta Conference in 1945. At Tehran, Roosevelt acted as a mediator between Churchill and Stalin, successfully advocating for Operation Overlord, the cross-Channel invasion of France. He maintained a working relationship with Stalin, believing personal diplomacy could ease postwar tensions. By Yalta, the alliance was strained, but Roosevelt still hoped cooperation could guide the postwar order. Discussions focused on dividing Germany, establishing the United Nations, and securing Soviet participation in the Pacific War. Roosevelt died shortly after Yalta and was succeeded by Harry S. Truman, who joined Churchill and Stalin at Potsdam. Roosevelt’s role in the Big Three was pivotal, balancing military necessities with a vision for a stable postwar world. His diplomatic style emphasized pragmatism and unity, helping hold together a complex alliance until victory was within reach.","faction":"USA","type":"order","rarity":"Rare","cost":5,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_the_big_three","title":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_the_big_three_title","SourceString":"THE BIG THREE","LocalizedString":"THE BIG THREE"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::USA","kredits":5,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_the_big_three_text","SourceString":"Each friendly unit gets +1+1 for each friendly unit not of the same nation.","LocalizedString":"Each friendly unit gets +1+1 for each friendly unit not of the same nation."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_the_big_three_history","SourceString":"The Big Three were the principal Allied leaders of WWII: Franklin D. Roosevelt, President of the USA, Winston Churchill, Prime Minister of the United Kingdom, and Joseph Stalin,General Secretary of the Soviet Union. From 1941, when the US entered the war, Roosevelt pushed for unity among the Allies despite differing political systems and postwar goals. His leadership helped shape the strategic alliance, particularly during the Tehran Conference in 1943 and the Yalta Conference in 1945. At Tehran, Roosevelt acted as a mediator between Churchill and Stalin, successfully advocating for Operation Overlord, the cross-Channel invasion of France. He maintained a working relationship with Stalin, believing personal diplomacy could ease postwar tensions. By Yalta, the alliance was strained, but Roosevelt still hoped cooperation could guide the postwar order. Discussions focused on dividing Germany, establishing the United Nations, and securing Soviet participation in the Pacific War. Roosevelt died shortly after Yalta and was succeeded by Harry S. Truman, who joined Churchill and Stalin at Potsdam. Roosevelt’s role in the Big Three was pivotal, balancing military necessities with a vision for a stable postwar world. His diplomatic style emphasized pragmatism and unity, helping hold together a complex alliance until victory was within reach.","LocalizedString":"The Big Three were the principal Allied leaders of WWII: Franklin D. Roosevelt, President of the USA, Winston Churchill, Prime Minister of the United Kingdom, and Joseph Stalin,General Secretary of the Soviet Union. From 1941, when the US entered the war, Roosevelt pushed for unity among the Allies despite differing political systems and postwar goals. His leadership helped shape the strategic alliance, particularly during the Tehran Conference in 1943 and the Yalta Conference in 1945. At Tehran, Roosevelt acted as a mediator between Churchill and Stalin, successfully advocating for Operation Overlord, the cross-Channel invasion of France. He maintained a working relationship with Stalin, believing personal diplomacy could ease postwar tensions. By Yalta, the alliance was strained, but Roosevelt still hoped cooperation could guide the postwar order. Discussions focused on dividing Germany, establishing the United Nations, and securing Soviet participation in the Pacific War. Roosevelt died shortly after Yalta and was succeeded by Harry S. Truman, who joined Churchill and Stalin at Potsdam. Roosevelt’s role in the Big Three was pivotal, balancing military necessities with a vision for a stable postwar world. His diplomatic style emphasized pragmatism and unity, helping hold together a complex alliance until victory was within reach."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Rare","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::Homefront","soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'War_UI_Drum_Movement_Field_Snares_3'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/War_UI/Waves/Drum_Movements/War_UI_Drum_Movement_Field_Snares_3.0"},"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/USA/10_Homefront/T_the_big_three.T_the_big_three","SubPathString":""},"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/THE_BIG_THREE.THE_BIG_THREE","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_the_big_three_photo","SourceString":"The “Big Three” leaders at the Yalta conference, February 1945. ","LocalizedString":"The “Big Three” leaders at the Yalta conference, February 1945. "},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackOrder.BP_AttackOrder_C","SubP